// The `prelude.supervise` sub-module: the retry MECHANISM, split cleanly from the failure POLICY. A `supervise`
// provider re-runs the rest of a block, but it knows NOTHING about what counts as a retriable failure. It
// catches exactly one request — `interrupted` — and re-runs after its policy's delay. Deciding WHICH
// failures become an `interrupted` is user code: an ordinary `use handler` (a "converter") installed
// between the provider and the body that turns the throws / panics it chooses into `supervise.interrupted`.
//
//   use supervise.exponential(initial_delay_milliseconds = 1000, factor = 2, max_attempts = 5)
//   use handler {
//     // POLICY: this converter decides. Only transient errors become the signal; the rest rethrow.
//     request prelude.throw(error: fetch_error | auth_error) -> never {
//       match (error) {
//         case fetch_error(_) -> { supervise.interrupted(failure = error) }   // transient -> re-run
//         case auth_error(_)  -> { prelude.throw(error = error) }          // fatal     -> rethrow (proxies up)
//       }
//     }
//   }
//   <body>   // performs interrupted (via the converter); the provider sleeps and re-runs it
//
// ONE SIGNAL, TWO MECHANISMS. `interrupted[F]` is the whole seam, and there are exactly two useful things
// to do with it. RE-RUN the block — `immediate` / `forever` / `exponential`, the `use`-shaped providers
// above. Or DO NOT re-run it, and hand the signal back as a VALUE — `once`, below. The second is not a
// lesser retry policy; it is the other half of the module, and for a large class of failures it is the
// only correct one. An external call the runtime INTERRUPTED must never be re-run (the at-most-once
// rule), and a body that already had effects before it failed must not repeat them — so "post the reply,
// then fail" cannot be retried without posting twice. What those want is to learn WHAT HAPPENED and carry
// on, which is exactly `once`'s `ran | signalled`. The two mechanisms share the signal, the converter and
// the failure type `F`, so a block moves between them by changing one line.
//
// WHY THE SPLIT. The predecessor (`prelude.retry`) folded BOTH failure channels into the mechanism: its
// `attempt` caught every `throw` and every `panic` indiscriminately, so `retry.forever` spun forever on a
// revoked-credential `auth_error` exactly as on a transient `fetch_error` — there was no way to retry one
// and escalate the other. Moving the catch OUT of the mechanism and into a user converter makes selective
// retry an ordinary `match`, makes retrying a `panic` an explicit opt-in in source, and dissolves the old
// "a caught panic cannot be re-raised from Katari" problem: by the time control reaches a provider every
// failure is already an `interrupted[F]` whose `F` the converter chose as a value, so exhaustion re-raises
// a TYPED `throw[F]` and never needs to re-panic.
//
// THE COST MODEL — WHY EVERY LOOP HERE IS A `forever` AND NOT RECURSION. Calling an agent is a
// DELEGATION, and a delegation pushes a DURABLE frame that lives until the call returns. A loop written
// as self-recursion therefore never returns while it is looping: its depth is the number of iterations,
// and every one of those frames is persisted state the runtime carries for the rest of the run. Nothing
// reclaims them until the whole chain unwinds, so a long-lived recursive loop grows without bound — and
// a crash-recovery reload has to rebuild the chain. A `forever` loop is FLAT instead: each finished
// iteration's frames are reclaimed, and the loop's own `var`s are the only state that survives, however
// many times it has gone round. That is why the providers below are `forever` loops, why their durable
// footprint does not grow with the re-run count, and why a daemon polls with `time.watch` (whose reactor
// owns the loop, so a tick adds no depth) rather than a `time.sleep` recursion.
//
// The rule generalizes past this module: anything that iterates without a known-small bound wants a
// `forever` or an explicit worklist, not self-recursion. A read-only tree walk should drain a worklist
// breadth-first rather than recurse per node — a tool that leaves a durable frame chain behind has done
// more than read. Bounded, shallow recursion (a parser over a small document, a fixed-depth descent) is
// fine: the cost is per LIVE frame, not per call ever made.
//
// THE ONLY PERFORMABLE REQUEST IN THIS MODULE IS `interrupted` (the seam a converter performs). The
// providers are ordinary `forever` loops: their per-iteration `interrupted`-catch is `once`, and their
// evolving loop state (attempt count, backoff delay) is the `forever`'s own `var` — one owner, advanced by a
// `next … with (…)`. Loop EXIT is `break` (a lexical jump, not a request) and exhaustion is `prelude.throw`;
// there is NO internal loop-control request a block could perform to hijack the loop. `replay_after` /
// `give_up` are internal machinery (data the bounded loop matches on, none performable); they are module
// members only because Katari has no module-private declarations.

@"The supervision signal — the ONE request a `supervise` provider catches, and the ONLY performable request this
module exports. A converter performs it to ask that the enclosing block be re-run, carrying the failure it
chose to represent as @failure@ (of the converter's own type @F@). `-> never`: performing it transfers
control to the provider (which applies its delay policy and re-runs) and does not return. This is the whole
seam between policy and mechanism — a provider reasons only about `interrupted`, never about `throw` or
`panic`. Only a `supervise` provider installed inside the program serves it: the runtime never machine-answers
`interrupted`, so signal it only below a provider."
request interrupted[F](@"The converter-chosen value standing for the underlying failure (re-raised as `throw[F]` when a bounded provider exhausts)." failure: F) -> never

// ── THE CONVERTERS: the two failure channels, as `use` lines instead of hand-written handlers ─────
//
// A converter is policy, and policy belongs to the program — but the SHAPE of a converter is not policy,
// it is the same three lines every time: install a handler, name the failure, signal. These two write
// those lines. They convert TOTALLY (a handler must admit every failure the block can raise, so partial
// catching is not a thing a converter can do); SELECTION happens where it reads better anyway, in the
// `match` over `signalled`'s payload, as ordinary flat code with a `prelude.throw` arm for what should
// keep flying. They compose by stacking, and stacking is how one block covers both channels.

@"A panic reached a converter — an interrupted call under the at-most-once rule, or a defect. It carries
only @message@, because that is all a panic carries. It exists so `signal_panics` has a value to signal
WITH: a bare string would collide with a throw payload of the same type when both converters stack, and
a program folding both wants two arms it can tell apart."
data panicked(@"The panic message." message: string)

@"Turn every `throw[T]` from the rest of the block into the supervision signal, carrying the thrown payload
UNCHANGED as the failure. The payload is already the program's own value, so nothing is wrapped and no
`F` has to be invented: `signal_throws[my_error]` gives a `signalled[my_error]`.

It converts TOTALLY, and that is not a limitation to work around — a throw handler must admit everything
the block can throw, so a converter that caught only part of `T` could not exist. Let the whole union
through and decide afterwards: `case my_fatal(...) -> prelude.throw(error = it)` in the `match` over the
signal is the same decision, written flat, where the reader can see it beside the other endings.

Written `use supervise.signal_throws[my_error]()`. It fixes the failure type to `T`, so it does not
stack with `signal_panics`; a block folding both channels writes one converter over the union it wants."
agent signal_throws[T, R, effect E](
  @"The rest of the block (bound by `use`)." continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, prelude.throw[T], interrupted[T]},
) -> R with {...E, interrupted[T]} {
  use handler {
    request prelude.throw(error: T) -> never { interrupted(failure = error) }
  }
  continuation(value = null)
}

@"Turn every panic from the rest of the block into the supervision signal, carrying `panicked(message)`.

A panic is the channel a runtime restart arrives on: an external call the runtime interrupted is never
re-run, and the frame that was holding it panics. That is the single most common failure a resident
meets, and folding it is why `once` exists — so this is the line that turns \"the runtime restarted
under me\" into a value the program can answer.

RETRYING a panic is an explicit choice, not a default: under `once` the interrupted call is not re-run
(right for an interrupted call, and required for a body that already had effects), while under `forever`
or `exponential` it is. The converter is the same either way; the mechanism below it decides.

Written `use supervise.signal_panics()`. It fixes the failure type to `panicked`, so it does not stack
with `signal_throws`; a block folding both channels writes one converter over the union it wants."
agent signal_panics[R, effect E](
  @"The rest of the block (bound by `use`)." continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, interrupted[panicked]},
) -> R with {...E, interrupted[panicked]} {
  use handler {
    request panic(msg: string) { interrupted(failure = panicked(message = msg)) }
  }
  continuation(value = null)
}

// ── THE NON-REPLAYING MECHANISM: run it once, and answer with what happened ───────────────────────

@"One run that COMPLETED with @value@ — it did not signal."
data ran[R](@"The completed run's result." value: R)

@"One run that performed `interrupted`, carrying the @failure@ its converter chose."
data signalled[F](@"The failure the run's `interrupted` carried." failure: F)

@"Run @task@ ONCE and answer with which happened — `ran(value)` or `signalled(failure)`. The other half of
this module: where `forever` and `exponential` answer an `interrupted` by RE-RUNNING the block, this one
answers it by handing the failure back as a value, and the task is never re-entered.

REACH FOR IT WHENEVER RE-RUNNING WOULD BE WRONG, which is more often than it looks. An external call the
runtime interrupted must NOT be re-run — the at-most-once rule is the runtime's, not a preference — so a
turn, a send or a question that died with the process wants to be REPORTED, not retried. A body that
already had effects before it failed must not repeat them, so \"post the reply, then fail\" retries into a
double post. And a failure that will answer the same next time is not a retry candidate at all, only a
fact to fold. In every one of those the question is what happened, and the answer is this sum.

THE SHAPE THIS REPLACES is a guard whose only job is to convert: `use handler { request panic(msg) {
break my_arm(msg) } }` (or the `prelude.throw` twin) wrapped around one call, with the success case
constructed by hand — which forces a named agent per call site, a data type per failure, and a sum
per site to name the pair. Here the converter still chooses `F`, so the failure keeps the caller's own
vocabulary; what goes away is minting `ok` / `failed` constructors again at every boundary.

@F@ is inferred from @task@ and preserved into `signalled`; `interrupted[F]` sits in @task@'s OVERWRITE
row because this agent DISCHARGES it. It catches ONLY the supervision signal — never `throw`, never `panic`,
which is the converter's business and stays visible in the source."
agent once[R, F, effect E](
  @"The work to run once, with `interrupted` served for it. A converter installed inside it (or around this call) decides which throws and panics become the signal." task: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, interrupted[F]},
) -> ran[R] | signalled[F] with E {
  use handler {
    request interrupted(failure: F) -> never { break signalled(failure = failure) }
  }
  ran(value = task(value = null))
}

// THE REPLAYING MECHANISM — three delay policies over the one `once` boundary, each an ordinary `forever`
// loop. The loop's `var` is the SINGLE owner of the evolving state (backoff delay, attempt count); a
// `next … with (…)` advances it, a `break` carries the success value out, and durable footprint stays FLAT
// no matter how many times the block has replayed (every finished iteration's frames are reclaimed). The
// three differ only by their effect rows: `immediate` adds nothing, `forever` adds `io` (it sleeps),
// `exponential` adds `io` and `prelude.throw[F]` (it sleeps, then re-raises when the budget is spent).

@"Replay the rest of the block on every `interrupted`, unbounded and with NO artificial delay: it re-runs at
once. The retry cadence is therefore set entirely by whatever the converter does BEFORE it signals — e.g. a
converter that performs a human-in-the-loop escalation and parks before signalling gives the attended /
re-auth loop, human-paced with no polling. Never re-raises and never returns on its own; a converter that
rethrows instead of signalling is how a failure leaves this loop. Written `use supervise.immediate()`."
agent immediate[R, F, effect E](
  @"The rest of the block (bound by `use`)." continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, interrupted[F]},
) -> R with E {
  forever {
    match (once(task = continuation)) {
      case ran(value => value) -> { break value }   // success: carry it out of the loop
      case signalled(_) -> { null }                 // signalled: fall through, re-run at once
    }
  }
}

@"Replay the rest of the block on every `interrupted`, unbounded, with exponential backoff capped at
@max_delay_milliseconds@. For daemons that must stay up across transient failures (e.g. keeping a
`time.watch` alive): it never re-raises and never returns on its own. The backoff delay lives in the loop's
own @delay@ var and grows by @factor@ per re-run up to the cap (`math.min`), so the durable state stays flat
across any number of replays. Written
`use supervise.forever(initial_delay_milliseconds = ..., factor = ..., max_delay_milliseconds = ...)`.
SUPERVISING A REGION: a converter cannot see a FIBER's failure. A fiber's panic is delivered to the region
as the typed `region.crashed` event rather than unwinding past `region.watch`, so a resident whose work runs
in fibers must perform `interrupted` from its `region.crashed` clause (installed inside this scope) — a
`panic` converter alone catches only the failures raised on the block's own path. What a re-run REBUILDS
is what the block installs: state a handler above this `use` owns survives every attempt, so install a desk
whose contents must outlive a reconnect above the scope, and whatever consumes that state on the same side
of the boundary."
agent forever[R, F, effect E](
  @"The delay before the first re-run, in milliseconds." initial_delay_milliseconds: number,
  @"The delay multiplier (e.g. 2 doubles the delay on each re-run)." factor: number,
  @"The backoff ceiling, in milliseconds; the delay never grows past it." max_delay_milliseconds: number,
  @"The rest of the block (bound by `use`)." continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, interrupted[F]},
) -> R with E | io {
  forever (var delay = initial_delay_milliseconds) {
    match (once(task = continuation)) {
      case ran(value => value) -> { break value }
      case signalled(_) -> {
        time.sleep(milliseconds = delay)
        next with { delay = math.min(left = delay * factor, right = max_delay_milliseconds) }
      }
    }
  }
}

@"A bounded-retry decision: keep going after sleeping the current delay."
data replay_after()

@"The exhausted arm of a bounded-retry decision: no attempts remain, so the provider re-raises the failure."
data give_up()

@"Replay the rest of the block on `interrupted` with exponential backoff, up to @max_attempts@ times. On
failure number n (1-based) with n < @max_attempts@: durably sleep @initial_delay_milliseconds@ *
@factor@^(n-1), then re-run. When the budget is spent (the @max_attempts@-th `interrupted`): re-raise that
failure as a TYPED `throw[F]` — @F@ is the converter's chosen failure representation, so exhaustion keeps the
error typed end to end and never needs to re-panic. The attempt count and delay live in the loop's own vars
(one owner). The exhausted-vs-continue decision is genuine sum dispatch: the ONE comparison of the count to
the budget mints a `replay_after | give_up`, and the `signalled` arm `match`es it once — no code re-tests the
counter. Written `use supervise.exponential(initial_delay_milliseconds = ..., factor = ..., max_attempts = ...)`."
agent exponential[R, F, effect E](
  @"The delay before the first re-run, in milliseconds." initial_delay_milliseconds: number,
  @"The delay multiplier (e.g. 2 doubles the delay on each re-run)." factor: number,
  @"The total run budget, not a retry count: the @max_attempts@-th `interrupted` re-raises instead of re-running." max_attempts: number,
  @"The rest of the block (bound by `use`)." continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, interrupted[F]},
) -> R with E | io | prelude.throw[F] {
  forever (var attempt = 0, var delay = initial_delay_milliseconds) {
    match (once(task = continuation)) {
      case ran(value => value) -> { break value }
      case signalled(failure => failure) -> {
        // THE one comparison boundary: classify the running failure count against the budget into a
        // `replay_after | give_up` sum ONCE, then dispatch on the sum.
        let verdict = if (attempt + 1 < max_attempts) { replay_after() } else { give_up() }
        match (verdict) {
          case replay_after(_) -> {
            time.sleep(milliseconds = delay)
            next with { attempt = attempt + 1, delay = delay * factor }
          }
          case give_up(_) -> { prelude.throw(error = failure) }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
